\mt1 James
\c 1
\s1 A Greeting from James
\r (\ref Jude 1:1–2|JUD 1-2\ref*)
\p \v 1 James, a servant of God and of \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ,
\p To the twelve tribes of the Dispersion:\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Literally \fqa To the twelve tribes in the Diaspora\ft . Originally referring to the Jewish people living outside the land of Israel, \fqa the Diaspora \ft is applied here to the Jewish believers scattered abroad.\f*
\p Greetings.
\s1 Rejoicing in Trials
\r (\ref Philippians 1:12–20|PHP 1:12-20\ref*)
\p \v 2 Consider \add it\add* pure joy, my brothers, when you encounter trials of many kinds, \v 3 because you know that the testing of your faith develops perseverance. \v 4 Allow perseverance to finish \add its\add* work, so that you may be mature and complete, \add not\add* lacking \add anything\add*.
\p \v 5 Now if any of you lacks wisdom, he should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to him. \v 6 But he must ask in faith, without doubting, because he who doubts is like a wave of \add the\add* sea, blown and tossed by the wind. \v 7 That man \add should\add* not expect to receive anything from the Lord. \v 8 \add He is\add* a double-minded man, unstable in all his ways.
\p \v 9 The brother in humble circumstances should exult in his high position. \v 10 But the \add one who is\add* rich \add should exult\add* in his low position, because he will pass away like a flower of the field. \v 11 For the sun rises with scorching heat and withers the plant; its flower falls and \add its\add* beauty is lost. So too, the rich \add man\add* will fade away in the midst of his pursuits.
\p \v 12 Blessed \add is the\add* man who perseveres under trial, because when he has stood the test, he will receive the crown of life that \add God\add* has promised to those who love Him.
\s1 Good and Perfect Gifts
\p \v 13 When tempted, no one should say, “God is tempting me.” For God cannot be tempted by evil, \add nor\add* does He tempt \add anyone\add*. \v 14 But each one is tempted \add when\add* by \add his\add* own evil desires he is lured away and enticed. \v 15 Then \add after\add* desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin; and sin, when it is full-grown, gives birth to death.
\p \v 16 \add Do\add* not be deceived, my beloved brothers. \v 17 Every good and perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of the heavenly lights, with whom there is no change or shifting shadow.\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Or \fqa no change or shadow of turning.\f* \v 18 He chose to give us birth through \add the\add* word of truth, that we would be a kind of firstfruits of His creation.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Or \fqa of His creatures.\f*
\s1 Hearing and Doing
\p \v 19 My beloved brothers, understand this: Everyone should be quick to listen, slow to speak, \add and\add* slow to anger, \v 20 for man’s anger \add does\add* not bring about \add the\add* righteousness that God \add desires\add*. \v 21 Therefore, get rid of all moral filth and every expression of evil, \add and\add* humbly accept the word planted \add in you\add*, which can save your souls.
\p \v 22 Be doers of \add the\add* word, and not hearers only. \add Otherwise,\add* you are deceiving yourselves. \v 23 For anyone who hears \add the\add* word but does not carry it out is like a man \add who\add* looks at his face in a mirror, \v 24 \add and\add* after observing himself goes away and immediately forgets what he \add looks\add* like. \v 25 But the \add one who\add* looks intently into \add the\add* perfect law of freedom, and continues to do so — not being a forgetful hearer, but an effective doer — \add he\add* will be blessed in what he does.
\p \v 26 If anyone considers himself religious \add and yet\add* does not bridle his tongue, he deceives his heart \add and his\add* religion \add is\add* worthless. \v 27 Pure and undefiled religion before \add our\add* God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress, \add and\add* to keep oneself from being polluted by the world.
\c 2
\s1 A Warning against Favoritism
\p \v 1 My brothers, as you hold out \add your\add* faith in our glorious Lord Jesus Christ, \add do\add* not \add show\add* favoritism.
\p \v 2 Suppose a man comes into your meeting\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Greek \fqa your synagogue\f* \add wearing\add* a gold ring \add and\add* fine clothes, and a poor \add man\add* in shabby clothes also comes in. \v 3 \add If\add* you lavish attention on the \add man\add* in fine clothes and say, “Here \add is\add* a seat of honor,” \add but\add* say to the poor \add man\add*, “You must stand” or “Sit at my feet,” \v 4 have you not discriminated among yourselves and become judges \add with\add* evil thoughts?
\p \v 5 Listen, my beloved brothers: \add Has\add* not God chosen the poor of this world \add to be\add* rich in faith and to inherit the kingdom He promised those who love Him? \v 6 But you have dishonored the poor. \add Is it\add* not the rich who oppress you and drag you into court? \v 7 \add Are\add* they not the ones who blaspheme the noble name by which you have been called?\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Or \fqa the noble name invoked upon you \ft or \fqa the noble name of Him to whom you belong\f*
\p \v 8 If you really fulfill \add the\add* royal law stated in Scripture, “Love your neighbor as yourself,”\f + \fr 2:8 \ref Leviticus 19:18|LEV 19:18\ref*\f* you are doing well. \v 9 But if you show favoritism, you sin \add and\add* are convicted by the law as transgressors.
\p \v 10 Whoever keeps the whole law but stumbles at just one \add point\add* is guilty of \add breaking\add* all of it. \v 11 For He who said, “Do not commit adultery,”\f + \fr 2:11 \ref Exodus 20:14|EXO 20:14\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:18\f* also said, “Do not murder.”\f + \fr 2:11 \ref Exodus 20:13|EXO 20:13\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:17\f* If you do not commit adultery, but do commit murder, you have become a lawbreaker.
\p \v 12 Speak and act as those who are going to be judged by \add the\add* law that gives freedom. \v 13 For judgment without mercy  \add will be shown to \add* anyone who has not been merciful. Mercy triumphs over judgment.
\s1 Faith and Works
\r (\ref Galatians 3:1–9|GAL 3:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 14 What good \add is it\add*, my brothers, if someone claims to have faith, but has no deeds? Can such faith save him? \v 15 Suppose a brother or sister is without clothes and daily food. \v 16 \add If\add* one of you tells him, “Go in peace; stay warm and well fed,” but \add does\add* not provide for \add his\add* physical needs, what good \add is that \add*? \v 17 So too, faith by itself, if it does not result in action,\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Literally \fqa if it does not have works\f* is dead.
\p \v 18 But someone will say, “You have faith and I have deeds.” Show me your faith without deeds, and I will show you \add my\add* faith by my deeds. \v 19 You believe that God is one.\f + \fr 2:19 \ft Or \fqa that there is one God\f* Good for you! Even the demons believe \add that\add* — and shudder.
\p \v 20 O foolish man, do you want evidence that faith without deeds is worthless?\f + \fr 2:20 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa dead\f* \v 21 \add Was\add* not our father Abraham justified by what he did when he offered his son Isaac on the altar? \v 22 You see that \add his\add* faith was working with his actions, and \add his\add* faith was perfected by what he did. \v 23 And the Scripture was fulfilled that says, “Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness,”\f + \fr 2:23 \ref Genesis 15:6|GEN 15:6\ref*\f* and he was called a friend of God.\f + \fr 2:23 \ft See \ref Isaiah 41:8|ISA 41:8\ref*.\f* \v 24 As you can see, a man is justified by \add his\add* deeds and not by faith alone.
\p \v 25 In the same way, \add was\add* not even Rahab the prostitute justified by her actions when she welcomed the spies\f + \fr 2:25 \ft Literally \fqa messengers\f* and sent \add them\add* off on another route? \v 26 As the body without \add the\add* spirit is dead, so faith without deeds is dead.
\c 3
\s1 Taming the Tongue
\r (\ref Psalm 64:1–10|PSA 64:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 1 Not many \add of you\add* should become teachers, my brothers, \add because\add* you know that we \add who teach \add* will be judged more strictly. \v 2 We all stumble in many ways. If anyone \add is\add* never at fault in what he says, he \add is\add* a perfect man, able to control \add his\add* whole body.
\p \v 3 \add When\add* we put bits into the mouths of horses to make them obey us, we can guide the whole animal. \v 4 Consider ships as well. Although they are so large and are driven by strong winds, they are steered by a very small rudder wherever the pilot is inclined.
\p \v 5 In the same way, the tongue is a small part of the body, \add but\add* it boasts of great things. Consider how small a spark sets a great forest \add ablaze\add*. \v 6 The tongue also \add is\add* a fire, a world of wickedness among the parts of the \add body\add*. It pollutes the whole person, sets the course of his life \add on fire\add*, and is itself set on fire by hell.\f + \fr 3:6 \ft Greek \fqa Gehenna\f*
\p \v 7 All kinds of animals, birds, reptiles, and creatures of the sea are being tamed and have been tamed by man, \v 8 but no man can tame the tongue. \add It is\add* a restless evil, full of deadly poison.
\p \v 9 With \add the tongue\add* we bless our Lord and Father, and with it we curse men, who have been made in God’s likeness. \v 10 Out of the same mouth come blessing and cursing. My brothers, this should not be! \v 11 \add Can both\add* fresh \add water\add* and salt \add water\add*\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Literally \fqa Can both fresh and bitter\f* flow from the same spring? \v 12 My brothers, can a fig tree grow olives, or a grapevine \add bear\add* figs? Neither \add can\add* a salt \add spring\add*\f + \fr 3:12 \ft Literally \fqa Neither can salt\f* produce fresh water.
\s1 The Wisdom from Above
\p \v 13 Who \add is\add* wise and understanding among you? Let him show it by \add his\add* good conduct, \add by\add* deeds \add done\add* in \add the\add* humility that comes from wisdom. \v 14 But if you \add harbor\add* bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your hearts, \add do\add* not boast \add in it\add* or deny the truth. \v 15 Such wisdom \add does\add* not come from above, but \add is\add* earthly, unspiritual, demonic. \v 16 For where jealousy and selfish ambition \add exist\add*, there \add will be \add* disorder and every evil practice.
\p \v 17 But \add the\add* wisdom from above is first \add of all\add* pure, then peace-loving, gentle, accommodating, full of mercy and good fruit, impartial, \add and\add* sincere. \v 18 Peacemakers \add who\add* sow in peace \add reap  the\add* fruit of righteousness.\f + \fr 3:18 \ft Literally \fqa But the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace by those making peace.\f*
\c 4
\s1 A Warning against Pride
\p \v 1 What \add causes\add* conflicts and quarrels among you? Don’t \add they come\add* from \add the\add* passions at war within you?\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Literally \fqa passions warring among your members?\f* \v 2 You crave \add what you do\add* not have; you kill and covet, but are unable to obtain \add it\add*. You quarrel and fight. You do not have, because you \add do\add* not ask. \v 3 \add And when\add* you do ask, \add you do\add* not receive, because you ask with wrong motives, that you may squander \add it\add* on your pleasures.
\p \v 4 \add You\add* adulteresses!\f + \fr 4:4 \ft See \ref Hosea 3:1|HOS 3:1\ref*.\f* Do you not know that friendship with the world is hostility \add toward\add* God? Therefore, whoever chooses to be a friend of the world renders himself\f + \fr 4:4 \ft Or \fqa is appointed\f* an enemy of God. \v 5 Or do you think the Scripture says without reason \add that\add* the Spirit\f + \fr 4:5 \ft Or \fqa the spirit\f* He caused to dwell in us yearns with envy? \v 6 But He gives \add us\add* more grace. This is why it says:
\b 
\q1 “God opposes \add the\add* proud,
\q2 but gives grace to \add the\add* humble.”\f + \fr 4:6 \ref Proverbs 3:34|PRO 3:34\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 Drawing Near to God
\p \v 7 Submit yourselves, then, to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you. \v 8 Draw near to God, and He will draw near to you. Cleanse \add your\add* hands, \add you\add* sinners, and purify \add your\add* hearts, \add you\add* double-minded. \v 9 Grieve, mourn, and weep. Turn your laughter to mourning, and \add your\add* joy to gloom. \v 10 Humble yourselves before \add the\add* Lord, and He will exalt you.
\p \v 11 Brothers, \add do\add* not slander one another. Anyone who speaks against \add his\add* brother or judges \add him\add* speaks against \add the\add* law and judges \add it\add*. And if you judge \add the\add* law, you are not a practitioner of \add the\add* law, but a judge \add of it\add*. \v 12 There is only one Lawgiver and Judge, the \add One who\add* is able to save and destroy. But who are you to judge \add your\add* neighbor?
\s1 Do Not Boast about Tomorrow
\r (\ref Proverbs 27:1|PRO 27:1\ref*)
\p \v 13 Come now, you who say, “Today or tomorrow we will go to this or that city, spend a year there, carry on business, and make a profit.” \v 14 You do not even know what \add will happen\add* tomorrow! What \add is\add* your life? You are a mist that appears for a little \add while\add* and then vanishes.
\p \v 15 Instead, you \add ought\add* to say, “If the Lord is willing, we will live and do this or that.” \v 16 As it is, you boast in your proud intentions. All such boasting is evil. \v 17 Anyone, then, who knows \add the\add* right thing to do, yet fails to do \add it\add*, is guilty of sin.
\c 5
\s1 A Warning to the Rich
\r (\ref Proverbs 23:1–5|PRO 23:1-5\ref*; \ref 1 Timothy 6:17–19|1TI 6:17-19\ref*)
\p \v 1 Come now, \add you who are\add* rich, weep \add and\add* wail over the misery to come upon you. \v 2 Your riches have rotted and moths have eaten your clothes. \v 3 Your gold and silver are corroded. Their corrosion will testify against you and consume your flesh like fire.
\p You have hoarded treasure in \add the\add* last days. \v 4 Look, the wages you withheld from the workmen who mowed your fields are crying out against you. The cries of the harvesters have reached the ears of \add the\add* Lord of Hosts.
\p \v 5 \add You have lived\add* on earth in luxury and self-indulgence. You have fattened your hearts in \add the\add* day of slaughter. \v 6 You have condemned \add and\add* murdered the righteous, who did not resist you.
\s1 Patience in Suffering
\r (\ref Job 1:1–5|JOB 1:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 7 Be patient, then, brothers, until the Lord’s coming. See how the farmer awaits the precious fruit of the soil — how patient he is for \add the fall\add* and \add spring rains\add*.\f + \fr 5:7 \ft Literally \fqa for it until it receives the early and the late\f* \v 8 You, too, be patient \add and\add* strengthen your hearts, because the Lord’s coming is near. \v 9 \add Do\add* not complain about one another, brothers, so that you will not be judged. Look, the Judge is standing at the door!
\p \v 10 Brothers, \add as\add* an example of patience in affliction, take the prophets who spoke in the name of \add the\add* Lord. \v 11 See how blessed we consider those who have persevered. You have heard of Job’s perseverance and have seen the outcome from \add the\add* Lord. The Lord is full of compassion and mercy.
\p \v 12 Above all, my brothers, \add do\add* not swear, not \add by\add* heaven \add or\add* earth \add or by\add* any other oath. Simply \add let\add* your “Yes” be yes, and \add your\add* “No,” no, so that you will not fall under judgment.
\s1 The Prayer of Faith
\p \v 13 \add Is\add* any one of you suffering? He should pray. \add Is\add* anyone cheerful? He should sing praises. \v 14 \add Is\add* any one of you sick? He should call the elders of the church to pray over him and anoint him with oil in the name of the Lord. \v 15 And the prayer offered in faith will restore the one who is sick. The Lord will raise him up. If he has sinned, he will be forgiven.
\p \v 16 Therefore confess \add your\add* sins to each other and pray for each other so that you may be healed. \add The\add* prayer of a righteous \add man\add* has great power to prevail. \v 17 Elijah was a man just like us. He prayed earnestly \add that it would\add* not rain, and it did not rain on the land for three and \add a half\add* years. \v 18 Again he prayed, and the heavens gave rain, and the earth yielded its crops.
\s1 Restoring a Sinner
\p \v 19 My brothers, if one of you should wander from the truth and someone should bring him back, \v 20 consider this: Whoever turns a sinner from \add the\add* error of his way will save his soul from death and cover over a multitude of sins.
